INT. DECOMMISSIONED GRAIN ELEVATOR - NIGHT

A concrete cathedral of silos, six stories of rust and pigeon-dung, stairwells winding upward like the inside of a seashell. Moonlight falls through a broken roof panel and lays a cold coin on the floor.

DETECTIVE IMOGEN KARLSSON-OYELARAN (40s, rain-colored coat, a limp she disguises as a stride) climbs the catwalk with a flashlight held low. Her breath fogs.

Below, in the shadow of Silo Three, MAXIMILIAN "MAXI" BRZEZINSKI (30s, bookish, a scholar's stoop, hands trembling around a duffel bag) watches her ascend.

A radio crackles on her belt.

DISPATCHER (V.O.)
(over radio)
Karlsson, backup is eleven minutes out.

IMOGEN
Eleven's too long.

DISPATCHER (V.O.)
(over radio)
Say again?

She switches the radio off. Above her, a boot scrapes iron.

On the upper gantry, RAFFERTY DUNNE (50s, heavy, a gold tooth that catches the moon) and his niece, SOLENNE DUNNE (20s, shaved head, a ledger tucked under one arm) lean over the rail, perfectly still.

Far across the chamber, ABEBE TSEGAYE (60s, retired elevator operator, a ring of keys at his belt) stands at the old control panel, one hand on a lever.

ABEBE
(calling out)
Everyone stays where they are.

RAFFERTY
Detective, you're early.

IMOGEN
Mr. Dunne, put the ledger down.

SOLENNE
It's not his ledger.

RAFFERTY
It's not her ledger.

MAXI ^
(from below)
It's mine!

The echo carries the word up through six stories of concrete and back down again, thinner each time.

Abebe's hand tightens on the lever. Somewhere beneath the floor, a conveyor chain groans awake.
